      "biography": "Dreadzone are a British dub and electronic group formed in London in 1993 by Greg Roberts (Greg Dread), formerly of Big Audio Dynamite, bassist Leo Williams, and producer Tim Bran, with the name suggested by DJ and filmmaker Don Letts. Their music combines dub and reggae foundations with house, techno, breakbeat, rock, folk, and live-band energy, creating a distinctive sound associated with the post-rave British dance scene. They gained early attention through festival performances, including opening Glastonbury’s Pyramid Stage in 1994, and through John Peel’s support on BBC Radio 1.\n\nThe group’s debut, 360°, appeared in 1993 on Creation Records, followed by the influential Second Light in 1995, which brought Dreadzone wider recognition and UK chart success. Subsequent releases include Biological Radio (1997), Sound (2001), Once Upon a Time (2005), Eye on the Horizon (2010), Escapades (2013), Dread Times (2017), and Nine (2024). Through more than three decades of recording, touring, and their Dreadzone Soundsystem events, the band have helped connect dub and reggae traditions with contemporary British electronic music while remaining celebrated for their powerful live performances.",
